1. Project Overview
Overarching Goal

The overarching goal of this project is to characterize age-associated molecular signatures of corpora amylacea (CA) and to determine whether spatial transcriptomic profiles surrounding CA can be used to distinguish CA-associated tissue from non-CA tissue. Ultimately, the project aims to assess whether these spatial gene expression patterns can support the development of a classifier that identifies CA-associated microenvironments.

Rationale

Corpora amylacea are age-associated, glycoprotein-rich aggregates commonly observed in the aging human brain and in neurodegenerative conditions. Despite their frequent presence, their biological significance and molecular context remain poorly understood. Understanding the transcriptional landscape surrounding CA may reveal insights into aging-related processes such as altered metabolism, inflammation, or cellular stress responses. This project addresses the gap between histological observation of CA and their molecular and spatial context, making it a worthwhile and timely problem in aging and neurobiology research.

Specific Aims
Aim 1: Identify age-associated transcriptional signatures in spatial regions proximal to corpora amylacea

Aim statement: Quantify and compare gene expression patterns in spatial transcriptomic spots located near corpora amylacea versus regions distal to CA across age groups.

Expected outcomes: Identification of genes and pathways enriched in CA-adjacent regions, potentially related to aging, glial activity, metabolic stress, or protein aggregation.

Potential challenges: Spatial resolution may blur CA-adjacent and non-adjacent regions. This will be addressed by testing multiple distance thresholds and aggregating spots into defined spatial bins.

Aim 2: Build and evaluate a classifier to distinguish CA-associated regions using spatial transcriptomic data

Aim statement: Develop a machine learning classifier that predicts whether a spatial transcriptomic region is CA-associated based on gene expression features.

Expected outcomes: A trained model that can accurately classify CA-proximal versus CA-distal regions and highlight predictive gene features.

Potential challenges: Class imbalance (fewer CA-associated regions) and overfitting. These will be addressed using balanced sampling strategies, regularization, and cross-validation.

2. Data
Dataset Description

Source: Publicly available or lab-generated spatial transcriptomics datasets (e.g., 10x Visium) from human brain tissue across different ages, paired with histological annotations identifying corpora amylacea.

Size:

Samples: Multiple tissue sections across individuals and age groups

Features: ~10,000–20,000 genes per spatial spot

Storage: On the order of tens of gigabytes depending on image resolution and number of samples

Format:

Gene expression matrices (HDF5 / CSV)

Spatial coordinates (CSV / JSON)

Histology images (TIFF / PNG)

Data Suitability

The raw data format is suitable but requires preprocessing.

Necessary steps include normalization, log transformation, filtering low-quality spots, and spatial annotation of CA proximity.

Spatial distances from CA regions will be computed to define CA-adjacent versus distal spots.

Storage and Data Management

Storage: Data will be stored on a secure institutional server or HPC filesystem.

Backup: Automated backups using institutional data management systems or cloud-based redundancy.

Sharing: Processed datasets and metadata will be shared with collaborators via controlled-access repositories (e.g., GitHub + cloud storage or institutional data portals).

3. Environment
Coding Environment

Analysis will be conducted using Jupyter notebooks on a local machine and/or HPC cluster, depending on dataset size.

Development may also use VS Code or code-server for remote work.

Dependencies

Key tools and libraries include:

Python (NumPy, pandas, SciPy)

Scanpy / Squidpy for spatial transcriptomics

scikit-learn for machine learning

matplotlib / seaborn for visualization

Reproducibility

Version control: Git and GitHub

Environment management: requirements.txt or environment.yml

Optional containerization: Docker or Singularity for portability and reproducibility

4. Pipeline
Algorithms and Methods

Quality control and filtering of spatial transcriptomic spots

Normalization and transformation of gene expression data

Annotation of spatial regions relative to corpora amylacea

Differential expression analysis between CA-adjacent and CA-distal regions

Feature selection for machine learning

Classifier training, validation, and evaluation

Each step depends on outputs from the previous step, particularly spatial annotation and preprocessing.

Scalability and Efficiency

Sparse matrix representations and vectorized operations will be used

Dimensionality reduction and feature selection will limit computational load

HPC parallelization may be used for large datasets or parameter sweeps

5. Machine Learning
Task Definition

This is a supervised classification task: predicting whether a spatial transcriptomic region is CA-associated or non-CA-associated based on gene expression data.

Feature Representation

Normalized gene expression values

Optionally reduced via PCA or restricted to biologically relevant gene sets

Spatial features (e.g., distance to CA) may be included as auxiliary inputs

Model Selection

Baseline: Logistic regression

Advanced models: Random forest, support vector machine, or gradient boosting
These models balance interpretability and performance for high-dimensional biological data.

Generalization Strategy

Train/validation/test splits across tissue sections or individuals

k-fold cross-validation

Regularization and early stopping to prevent overfitting

Evaluation Metrics

Accuracy

Precision, recall, and F1-score (important for class imbalance)

ROC-AUC to evaluate discrimination performance
